Subject: [PATCH] ext4: reuse free inodes immediately without a journal

Without a journal, find_inode_bit() starts at bit 0 of the group on every
allocation and calls recently_deleted() for each free inode it meets. It
skips inodes deleted in the last 60 seconds, or 360 seconds while their
inode-table block is dirty, and prefers the next free one. After a workload
deletes N files, each of the next creates in that group repeats those N
buffer-cache lookups, so create-after-delete costs O(creates * deletes).
In a SporeVM guest, creating 2,500 small files after earlier batches were
deleted took 330 to 1,220 ms instead of 14 ms.

The delay protects a filesystem that boots again after an unclean
shutdown and is repaired by e2fsck: a stale directory entry could then
name a reused inode. SporeVM never boots a journal-less disk that way.
Named VMs are live-only, checkpoints capture memory and disk together,
disk publication freezes the filesystem first, and unclean build-cache
disks are recreated. Allocate exactly as journaled ext4 does.

---
 fs/ext4/ialloc.c | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/fs/ext4/ialloc.c b/fs/ext4/ialloc.c
--- a/fs/ext4/ialloc.c
+++ b/fs/ext4/ialloc.c
@@ -673,7 +673,8 @@ static int find_group_other(struct super_block *sb, struct inode *parent,
 #define RECENTCY_MIN	60
 #define RECENTCY_DIRTY	300
 
-static int recently_deleted(struct super_block *sb, ext4_group_t group, int ino)
+static int __maybe_unused recently_deleted(struct super_block *sb,
+					   ext4_group_t group, int ino)
 {
 	struct ext4_group_desc	*gdp;
 	struct ext4_inode	*raw_inode;
@@ -725,7 +726,12 @@ static int recently_deleted(struct super_block *sb, ext4_group_t group, int ino)
 static int find_inode_bit(struct super_block *sb, ext4_group_t group,
 			  struct buffer_head *bitmap, unsigned long *ino)
 {
-	bool check_recently_deleted = EXT4_SB(sb)->s_journal == NULL;
+	/*
+	 * SporeVM: skipping recently deleted inodes rescans all of them on
+	 * every allocation, which makes create-after-delete quadratic. Reuse
+	 * free inodes as journaled ext4 does.
+	 */
+	bool check_recently_deleted = false;
 	unsigned long recently_deleted_ino = EXT4_INODES_PER_GROUP(sb);
 
